# M3 merge gate resolved: manus-parity fully merged, gates green
D2 gate fix complete: merge with manus-parity resolved keeping both sides, all gates green.

## The failure that was fixed
`.jgatefail.txt` reported: MERGE CONFLICT with latest manus-parity: run git merge
manus-parity, resolve all conflicts keeping both sides, commit.

The gate is not about a code defect. It asks for `manus-parity` to be a live
ancestor of this branch, so the merge had to be redone against whatever tip the
branch had at gate time. It advanced twice while this was being done, so two
merges were required rather than one.

## Merge 1 - parity tip 26befe6 (commit 330e135)
`git merge manus-parity` produced three conflicts:

- `src/protocol/events.ts` (content): HEAD imported `SimulationProgressEvent`;
  parity imported `RecipeUpdateEvent, RecipeRestartEvent`. Resolved as the union
  of both import lists.
- `src/protocol/types.ts` (content): the `ChatEvent` union. HEAD contributed
  `SimulationProgressEvent`; parity contributed `RecipeUpdateEvent,
  RecipeRestartEvent` alongside the shared `DeviceAttachmentUploadNotify`.
  Resolved keeping every member from both sides.
- `.jdone` (modify/delete): parity had deleted it, HEAD had modified it. Restored
  the branch version, which is the convention every sibling branch on this
  conflict uses (`jcode-j8` fdbe7f8, `h2-h4` 83191fb, `neo-n2` 634a23a).

Verified afterwards that all 4706 parity-only files were present, so the merge
dropped nothing.

## Merge 2 - parity tip 61aca0c (commit 7d8da0e)
Between the first merge and the gate re-check, `manus-parity` advanced 22 commits
(N9 ask-user node, J9 codemode, plus their own merge-gate fixes). The ancestor
check `git merge-base --is-ancestor manus-parity HEAD` correctly went false again,
so the new tip was merged. This time there were two conflicts, and
`src/protocol/events.ts` auto-merged:

- `src/protocol/types.ts` (content): HEAD now had `SimulationProgressEvent`; the
  new parity work added `AskUserRequestedEvent` and `AskUserResolvedEvent`.
  Resolved keeping all three, next to the shared
  `DeviceAttachmentUploadNotify`/`RecipeUpdateEvent`/`RecipeRestartEvent`.
- `.jdone` (add/add): parity now also tracks the file. Kept both sides - HEAD's M3
  conversational-wiring and regression reports, and parity's N9 done report plus
  its preserved rollup. Only the conflict markers were removed; no report text
  from either side was dropped.

After both merges: `git rev-list --left-right --count manus-parity...HEAD` is
`0 11`, and `git merge-base --is-ancestor manus-parity HEAD` exits 0. Every one
of the 4723 files at parity tip 61aca0c is present in the merged index.

## A real environmental test bug that the merge exposed
The full suite reported one failure that had nothing to do with the merge:
`src/server/__tests__/worktrees.test.ts` -> "reports non-git folders without
throwing".

Root cause: `readGitBranch` runs `git -C <dir> rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD`, and
git walks up to the nearest enclosing repository. `os.tmpdir()` on this machine
is `C:\Users\khamis\AppData\Local\Temp`, and `C:\Users\khamis` is itself a git
repository, so the "plain" temp folder was reported as git. Proved rather than
assumed: with `TEMP` pointed at a directory outside any repository the case
passes 4/4, and `git -C <tmpdir> rev-parse --show-toplevel` prints
`C:/Users/khamis`.

The test was asserting a property of the machine, not of the code. Fixed by
ceiling git's upward search at the temp root with `GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES`,
which is the convention already used for the identical assertion in
`src/tools/__tests__/codeSession.test.ts`. The assertions are unchanged and no
test was weakened or deleted. Committed as 5549aad.

## Gates (all run from the worktree root on the merged tree)

- `npx tsc --noEmit`                          -> exit 0
- `npm run build:server`                      -> exit 0 (dist/server.cjs 19.6mb, pdf worker copied)
- `(cd packages/web && npx next build)`        -> exit 0 (Compiled successfully)
- `node --import tsx --test "src/**/*.test.ts" "src/**/*.test.mjs"`
  -> tests 2634, suites 484, pass 2634, fail 0
- `(cd packages/web && npx vitest run)`        -> 79 files, 535 tests, all pass
- `npm run check:encoding`                     -> OK (no BOM, no mojibake, all JSON valid)
- `npm run check:modes`                        -> PASS
- `npm run check:eval`                         -> eval harness smoke: PASS
- `npm run check:recovery`                     -> PASS
- `npm run check:orchestrator`                 -> PASS (114/114)

No test was weakened or deleted; no assertion was relaxed to make the gate green.
No real external model API was called by any test.

## The regression that was found and fixed
The M3 async conversational surface did not route follow-ups. `asyncRunner.ts` classified with
`classifyWithSession(text, false)` and unconditionally ran a full simulation, so on the async path
(which is the live path whenever a provider client is present) every what-if / interview / roundtable /
A-B / auto-fix request started a **brand-new** simulation and overwrote the active session, instead of
answering against it. The synchronous surface routed all of these correctly, so the two surfaces had
silently diverged.

Proved before fixing, with a throwaway probe driving both surfaces against a deterministic fake
`ModelCaller`:

- sync  follow-up "what if we raise the price to 1300?" -> `what_if_injection` (correct)
- async follow-up "what if we raise the price to 1300?" -> `full_application_analysis` (wrong: ran a fresh sim)

## The fix
- `src/tools/mirofish.ts`: extracted `runMirofishFollowUp`, the single router for every
  session-bound branch (what-if, interview, roundtable, A/B, auto-fix, export, chaos, competitor,
  tests, time-travel, pricing, cross-project memory, compliance, viral GTM, red/blue, app synthesis,
  multi-region, podcast, MCTS, digital twin, NAS, formal verification, SLA, 3D). It returns `null`
  when the message is a full-analysis request. Both surfaces now call it first, so follow-up routing
  exists in exactly one place. `runConversationalMirofish` keeps its synchronous template full
  analysis (`runMirofishFullAnalysisSync`); `runConversationalMirofishAsync` falls through to the real
  swarm simulation. The async path passes only the caller's explicit `target` to the router, matching
  the synchronous behaviour (which guesses a target only inside the full-analysis branch).
- `src/tools/mirofish/asyncRunner.ts`: now reports `intent: "full_application_analysis"` instead of the
  raw classification, because follow-ups are routed before it is reached and it always runs a
  simulation. Previously unrelated text was labelled `unrelated` while a simulation really ran, which
  contradicted the synchronous surface.

## Behaviour preserved, proven not assumed
A 29-case routing battery (every follow-up family + full analysis + unrelated) was captured from the
synchronous surface **before** the refactor and re-captured after. The two maps are byte-identical
(`fc` clean), so the refactor changed no synchronous routing decision.

## Regression tests added (and proven to catch the bug)
`src/tools/mirofish/__tests__/asyncRunner.test.ts`:
- follow-ups reuse the active simulation and do not clobber the session (what-if, interview,
  roundtable, auto-fix)
- a full-analysis request still starts a fresh simulation
- a run the async surface actually executed is labelled `full_application_analysis`, matching sync

Negative control: with the router call temporarily disabled the new follow-up test fails
(`"what if we raise the price to 1300?" must route as a follow-up`), so the test genuinely guards the
regression rather than passing vacuously. The temporary hook was then reverted.

## Gates (run from the worktree root after the fix)
- `npx tsc --noEmit`                              -> exit 0
- `npm run check:encoding`                         -> OK (no BOM, no mojibake, all JSON valid)
- `npm run build:server`                           -> exit 0
- mirofish + protocol suites (asyncRunner, seed, simulate, intent, runner, http, loop, progress) -> tests 147, pass 147, fail 0
- `src/server/__tests__/*` + `src/agent/__tests__/*` -> tests 713, pass 711, fail 2

The two failures are outside this diff and pre-existing: `toolDescriptions.test.ts` (`suggest_plan`
pending-list defect, documented below) and `worktrees.test.ts` ("reports non-git folders without
throwing") which fails because `%TEMP%` resolves to `C:/Users/khamis`, itself inside a git repo, so
`readGitBranch` correctly reports `isGit: true`. Neither file references mirofish.

## Correction: the parity-ancestor claims in the preserved reports below are now stale
The preserved D2 / D10 / D19 reports below state that `git merge-base --is-ancestor manus-parity HEAD`
exits 0 with `rev-list --left-right --count manus-parity...HEAD = 0 N`. Those statements were true for
the merges they describe, but they are **no longer true of the current branch**: manus-parity has
advanced 78 commits since, so at HEAD `0138720`:

- `git merge-base --is-ancestor manus-parity HEAD` -> **exits 1** (not an ancestor)
- `git rev-list --left-right --count manus-parity...HEAD` -> **78  7**
- `git merge-base manus-parity HEAD` -> `b073763`

The historical reports are left as written (they record what was verified at the time); this note is
the authoritative current state.

