# N20 - Offline long-horizon eval gate for Neo: done

The gate is implemented, tested and recorded. All gates are green from the
worktree root on commit `7975ac8`.

## What N20 delivers

- `src/neoEval/harness.ts`: runs one fixture through the **production** agent
  loop (`runAgent`) with a scripted model. Produces the trace (steps, token
  usage, tool calls, injected failures, recovered failures, appends), runs the
  verifier script against the final answer, and scores it.
- `src/neoEval/mockModel.ts`: deterministic scripted model. Replays a
  `NeoScript` of `{ when, text, tool, citations }` turns, records every call
  (requestId, attempt, message/tool counts, prompt/completion tokens), and
  honours `when` guards by advancing the cursor past skipped turns.
- `src/neoEval/mockClock.ts`: deterministic clock (step count, elapsed ms).
- `src/neoEval/knowledge.ts`: seeded knowledge store + the citation extractor
  (numbered markers resolved against declared seeds).
- `src/neoEval/scoring.ts`: per-dimension scores (`completion`, `citation`,
  `flatness`, `recovery`, `recurring`) with evidence strings, plus
  `suiteResult` (pass bar 60, suite passes only when every case passes).
- `src/neoEval/types.ts`: the fixture/case/trace contracts and
  `NEO_HARNESS_VERSION`.
- `evals/neo/fixtures.ts`: the ten long-horizon fixtures, each targeting a
  distinct dimension (research-to-report, recurring-tracker,
  long-document-assembly, kb-grounded-answer, tool-failure-recovery,
  restart-from-step, step-budget, no-hallucinated-citations, deep-file-triage,
  uninterrupted-baseline).
- `src/scripts/eval-neo.ts`: the `npm run eval:neo` runner (`--list`, `--id`,
  `--json`, `--record`) and the baseline regression guard.
- `evals/neo/baseline.json`: the recorded baseline (see below).

## Baseline and regression guard

`evals/neo/baseline.json` is version-pinned to `NEO_HARNESS_VERSION`
(`neo-eval-2`): 10/10 cases pass, suite total 100, bar 60. `compareBaseline`
fails a run when a recorded case is missing, drops more than
`BASELINE_TOLERANCE` (1) point, falls below the bar, or the suite stops
clearing its own bar. A baseline recorded under a different harness version is
not compared (its numbers are not comparable), and the guard is skipped for a
single-fixture `--id` run. The version was bumped to `neo-eval-2` because the
flatness band and recurring check were corrected, so `neo-eval-1` scores are
not comparable.

## Real bugs the tests exposed (fixed, not worked around)

- `src/neoEval/harness.ts`: the harness's `observing` registry already injects
  failures, so the mock's `onCall` hook was double-injecting **and**
  double-recording calls. The mock is now built with `onCall: undefined`.
- `src/neoEval/scoring.ts`: added flatness noise tolerance
  (`flatToleranceRatio`, default 1.1) so small token wobble is not punished;
  `scoreRecurring` now reads observed appends from the trace instead of the
  fixture's own `count_min` expectation; `suiteResult([])` is now
  `failed: 1, verdict: "fail"` (an empty suite is not a pass); `round2` added
  to the flatness/recurring totals.
- `src/neoEval/mockModel.ts`: `nextStep()` now advances the cursor past every
  turn skipped by a `when` guard. Previously a guard that only became eligible
  after the next call left the script exhausted and returned empty text.

No test was weakened or deleted; no assertion was relaxed to make the gate
green.

## Gates (all run from the worktree root, on commit 7975ac8)

- `npx tsc --noEmit`                          -> exit 0
- `npm run eval:neo`                          -> 10/10 cases pass, total 100
- `node --import tsx --test src/neoEval/__tests__/{scoring,mockModel,harness,baseline}.test.ts`
  -> tests 88, pass 88, fail 0
- `npm run check:encoding` -> OK (no BOM, no mojibake, all JSON valid)
- `npm run check:eval`    -> eval harness smoke: PASS
- `npm run check:modes`   -> PASS
- `npm run check:recovery`-> PASS
- `npm run check:orchestrator` -> PASS
- `npm run build:server`  -> exit 0 (dist/server.cjs 19.2mb, pdf worker copied)

Working tree clean; no forbidden path touched; no real external model API was
called by the tests (the scripted `neo-mock` model is used throughout).

# C20 - Per-bot chat color (UI1): DONE

Each conversation now takes the color of the bot it belongs to. The user
bubble, send button and composer focus ring follow the companion's avatar
instead of the single global accent; a conversation with no bot keeps the
app accent exactly as before.

## What changed

New pure module `ui/chat-color.js` + `ui/chat-color.d.ts`
- `AVATAR_HEX` / `AVATAR_COLORS`: a hex for every server `AVATAR_COLORS`
  token (a test enforces the two stay in sync).
- `chatAccentFor(agent, {cache})`: (a) `avatarColor` token -> hex,
  (b) dominant color extracted client-side from `avatarImage` via a small
  canvas downsample (near-white / near-black / transparent pixels ignored),
  cached in `localStorage` under `loam.chatColor.<agentId>` keyed by a hash
  of the image URL, (c) deterministic hash of the agent id into the palette,
  (d) no agent -> `null`.
- `accentVars(hex, theme)`: `--chat-accent`, `--chat-accent-2`,
  `--chat-accent-ink`; a lightness ladder tuned per theme so the chosen ink
  clears WCAG AA 4.5:1 against the fill in both themes.
- Reuses the existing `readableInk` contrast helper rather than duplicating it.

Wiring
- `ui/app.js`: `applyChatAccent` resolves the task's agent and writes the vars
  on `#view-chat` only while a companion owns the task; it removes them
  otherwise and recomputes on the theme toggle.
- `ui/companions.js`: same shape on `.rk-main`, plus a `MutationObserver` on
  `data-theme` so the lightness/ink follow a theme change without a reload.

CSS (fallback pattern)
- `ui/loam.css`: `.primary` uses `var(--chat-accent-2,var(--accent-2))` /
  `var(--chat-accent,var(--accent))` and `color:var(--chat-accent-ink,white)`,
  plus chat-scoped `#view-chat[data-chat-accent="bot"] #sendChat`, composer
  focus and `.log-entry.user` overrides (needed because `ui/icons.css`
  `.loam-shell .primary` outranks a bare `.primary`).
- `ui/styles.css`: `.msg.user .user-bubble` falls back to the app accent.
- `ui/companions.html`: `.rk-msg.user .rk-bubble` falls back to the app accent;
  the companion send button and focus-within ring overrides sit at the end of
  the sheet so they out-rank `.rk-send:disabled` and the global focus rule.

## Verification

Gates, all run from the worktree root:
- `npx tsc --noEmit` -> exit 0
- `npm run build:server` -> exit 0
- `(cd packages/web && npx next build)` -> exit 0
- `node --import tsx --test src/__tests__/chatColor.test.ts src/design/__tests__/contrast.test.ts`
  -> tests 60, pass 60, fail 0
- `npm run check:encoding` -> OK (no BOM, no mojibake, all JSON valid)

Browser check (Playwright, both chat surfaces, view made visible first):
- task chat with a violet agent: send button gradient
  `rgb(139,123,216) -> rgb(166,152,226)`, ink `rgb(11,18,32)`, composer
  focus border `rgb(166,152,226)`, user entry border tinted. Without a bot
  every one of those falls back to the app accent.
- companion chat with the same agent: send button background
  `rgb(166,152,226)`, ink `rgb(11,18,32)`, accent border, and
  `.rk-bar:focus-within` ring all switch; without a bot they revert to the
  defaults.

Note: `.rk-send` carries `transition: background .12s`, so a measurement taken
in the same tick as the button becoming enabled reads the in-flight value.
The accent is present once the transition settles (~150 ms).

## Notes
- Pre-existing gate failures unrelated to this task (proved by stashing the C20
  work and re-running on the clean base, which fails identically):
  `check:loam-ui` asserts `#homeModes button` == 7 but finds 8, and the
  Playwright-based `check:loam-ancillary` / `check:isolation` time out. Neither
  touches a file this task changed.
- `ui/loam.css` was normalized to uniform CRLF to match every other file under
  `ui/` (it was the only mixed-EOL file in the tree).
- No forbidden path touched, no test weakened or deleted, no secrets committed.

## Round 2 - deeper validation (after the first pass was accepted)

A second pass drove the previously-untested runtime paths in a real browser
(Playwright) instead of only re-reading the code, and found one real defect.

### Defect found and fixed: the extracted avatar color was never painted

`chatAccentFor` samples an `avatarImage` asynchronously and returns the id-hash
fallback for the first paint, reporting the extracted color through the
`onAccent` callback. Both chat call sites called it **without** that callback:

- `ui/app.js` `applyChatAccent` (`#view-chat`)
- `ui/companions.js` `applyChatAccent` (`.rk-main`)

So on a cold cache (first time a bot with only an `avatarImage` was opened) the
conversation painted the id-hash fallback and the extracted avatar color was
never applied. It was only written to `localStorage`, so the correct color
appeared only after leaving and re-opening the conversation. A bot-less
conversation was unaffected, which is why the first pass (which used named-color
bots) did not catch it.

Fix (commit `f767cfa`): both call sites now pass `onAccent` and repaint as soon
as the sample lands. A monotonically increasing `applyChatAccentToken` per call
site drops a late sample that belongs to a conversation the user has already
left, so switching bots cannot be crossed by an out-of-order callback.

### What the deep probe confirmed (0 page errors, 0 console errors, 0 stub 404s)

- Real canvas extraction end to end: a hand-built 1x1 PNG served at
  `/avatar.png` (magenta `#c94fb0`) is sampled through the app's own
  `extractWithCanvas` path and painted as `#e09ad1` in dark, `#d16bbc` in light.
- Cold cache: selecting the image bot for the first time now paints the
  extracted color, not the id-hash fallback (`#f387bc`).
- Cache reuse: leaving for a hash bot (`#87f2e6`) and returning to the image bot
  restores the cached extracted color immediately, with no re-sample.
- Offline / ghost id: a task whose `agentId` is not in `/api/agents` still gets
  a deterministic id-hash color (`#98e1be`), matching the "offline: the id hash
  still colors the chat" fallback.
- Isolation: the accent lives only on `.rk-main` / `#view-chat`; `:root`,
  `body` and the sidebar carry no inline accent.
- Theme flip: the `MutationObserver` on `data-theme` recomputes lightness and
  ink in both themes (`#a698e2`/`#0b1220` dark, `#7760d2`/`#ffffff` light) and
  returns correctly to dark.
- Task chat: violet gradient `rgb(187,176,232) -> rgb(166,152,226)`, ink
  `rgb(11,18,32)`; a bot-less task leaves every accent property unset and falls
  back to the app accent.

### Gates re-run after the fix

- `npx tsc --noEmit` -> exit 0
- `npm run build:server` -> exit 0
- `node --import tsx --test src/__tests__/chatColor.test.ts src/design/__tests__/contrast.test.ts`
  -> tests 60, pass 60, fail 0
- `node --import tsx --test src/design/__tests__/*.test.ts`
  -> tests 171, pass 171, fail 0
- `npm run check:encoding` -> OK (no BOM, no mojibake, all JSON valid)
- `npm run check:modes`, `check:orchestrator` (114/114), `check:recovery`,
  `check:design-registry`, `check:promo-carousel`, `check:loam-agents` -> all pass

The deep probe was a scratch artifact and was removed before committing; the fix
itself is covered by the existing `chatColor.test.ts` async-callback tests plus
this browser-verified behaviour.

# C20 merge gate-fix session: merge with latest manus-parity, all gates green

`.jgatefail.txt` reported: `MERGE CONFLICT with latest manus-parity: run git merge
manus-parity, resolve all conflicts keeping both sides, commit.`

## Root cause
No source file conflicted on the first merge. `git merge manus-parity` (tip
`ed26550`, the N2 merge, bringing in N10/N2/N4/C19/O3/O6/O2/D5/O1/V11/H0/O0/fc-05/
J5/F5) auto-merged everything except the tracked rollup `.jdone`, where both sides
prepend a report at the same top-of-file anchor. Nothing in the C20 change set was
at risk: `git diff manus-parity -- ui/` still shows exactly the C20 files
(`ui/chat-color.js`, `ui/chat-color.d.ts`, `ui/app.js`, `ui/companions.js`,
`ui/companions.html`, `ui/loam.css`, `ui/styles.css`, 712 insertions / 9 deletions).

## What was done
Two merges were needed because `refs/heads/manus-parity` advanced twice while this
session ran.

1. **First merge** (parity tip `ed26550`, committed as `cd9ee76`). `.jdone` was
   resolved as a superset of both conflict sides. The base blob (`1d220ff4`) is a
   strict prefix of the ours blob (`f09b6564`), so the resolution keeps the base
   verbatim, then the C20 report (the part ours added), then a `---` separator, then
   the whole manus-parity rollup (`d58e75a3`) verbatim. Verified mechanically
   against both stage blobs: **0 non-empty lines missing from the ours side, 0
   non-empty lines missing from the theirs side**, and no conflict marker remains
   anywhere in the tree.

2. **Second merge** (parity tip `26befe6`, the N10 merge, committed as `c85f020`).
   The new conflict was a **modify/delete** on `.jdone`: N10's own commit `e3d05f6`
   had deleted the tracked rollup (698 lines removed) and the parity tip
   re-confirmed that deletion (`.jdone` is absent from `git ls-tree 26befe6`), while
   this branch still carried the full rollup. Resolved by **keeping our side** (the
   C20 branch's `.jdone`), because the deletion is a worktree-hygiene choice made on
   the N10 branch, not a content claim about the reports: every report the deletion
   would have dropped is still present verbatim in the resolved rollup, so nothing
   that either side wrote is lost. No other file conflicted.

3. **Third merge** (parity tip `3139f07`, the J9 revert, committed as `f017f96`).
   Parity advanced again in between (the J9 merge, then its revert). This merge
   applied cleanly with no conflict at all.

4. **Fourth merge** (parity tip `61aca0c`, the N9 merge, committed as `ecb7586`).
   N9 re-added `.jdone`, so the file conflicted again as an **add/add**: this
   branch's rollup (base + C20 reports) against parity's rollup (base + N9's
   Ask-User-More report). Both sides share a 5239-line suffix (the common
   rollup), so the resolution keeps ours verbatim, then a `---` separator, then
