J5 — Blast-radius shell command risk classifier: DONE

Summary
-------
Ports jcode's `command-risk` crate (MIT, `ref/command-risk/src/{lib,paths,tokenize,gate}.rs`)
to TypeScript under `src/policy/commandRisk`, and wires it in as **stage 1** of the
shell approval gate. The classifier is deliberately a *union* with the existing
`isDestructiveCommand` pattern denylist, never a replacement, so neither half can be
bypassed.

Implemented end to end:
  - Stage 1 (deterministic, high-recall):  src/policy/commandRisk/{tokenize,paths,lib}.ts
    - `tokenize`: shell-word tokenizer with quoting, `&&`/`||`/`;` segment splitting
      and embedded-substitution detection (a command hidden inside `$(...)`/backticks
      is still assessed, not skipped).
    - `paths`: `classifyTarget` / `expand` / `normalize` / `isCatastrophicTarget` /
      `isSafeRedirectSink` / `isTempPath` — resolves relative targets against the
      working dir and home dir and decides whether a write/delete target is
      catastrophic (workspace root, home, a whole drive, `/` , temp/scratch).
    - `lib`: `assess()` -> `RiskLevel` Safe | Low | Confirm | Catastrophic, with
      `maxLevel`, `severity`, `runsImmediately`, `isAbsoluteDeny`, `explanation`.
      Classifies by blast radius and reversibility: what the command would destroy
      and whether that is reversible.
  - Stage 2 (reflection, not a second model): src/policy/commandRisk/gate.ts
    - `gate()`: Safe/Low -> Allow; Catastrophic -> Deny (absolute, no justification
      unlocks it); Confirm -> Reflect (a refusal prompt that forces the *generating*
      model to name the user request it serves) unless the supplied `justification` is
      substantive (>=25 chars and not a bare affirmation such as "yes"/"ok"/"sure").
    - The refusal is not satisfiable by repetition: a blind retry of the identical
      call fails again.
  - Entry points:  src/policy/commandRisk/index.ts
    - `riskContextFromEnvironment(workingDir?, scratchDir?)`, `assessCommand(cmd, ctx)`,
      `checkCommandRisk(cmd, {workingDir, scratchDir, justification})` ->
      `{ ok, level, reason?, retryable }` (the seam the approval gate calls).

Wiring (union, not replacement):
  - `src/policy/riskTier.ts`: new `isBlastRadiusDestructive()` maps `Confirm` and
    `Catastrophic` to the `destructive` tier; `isDestructiveShell()` =
    `isDestructiveCommand(cmd) || isBlastRadiusDestructive(cmd, root)`. The pattern
    list catches what the structural classifier treats as opaque (`curl … | sh`,
    `reg add`, `git push`); the classifier catches what a name-based denylist cannot
    see (`find -delete`, `shred`, `dd of=`, `>file`, `sudo rm -rf ~`). Dropping either
    half would open a bypass. `classifyToolCall` now routes shell tools through
    `isDestructiveShell`.
  - `src/tools/shell.ts`: `shell_run` and `shell_write` call `checkCommandRisk` before
    the existing `checkCommand` substring denylist. `Catastrophic` returns a hard
    `blocked by policy`; `Confirm` returns a `requires approval` message telling the
    user to approve at the gate with a justification (a task-scoped rule cannot
    auto-approve it). Both gates run, so order only changes the message.
  - `THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES`: jcode (MIT) entry listing the adapted `commandRisk` files.

Acceptance criteria (AC) met
----------------------------
The port carries the upstream crate's own cases as fixtures (85 upstream cases) plus
25 Windows/PowerShell cases added here, all green:
  - a command whose blast radius cannot be checked in advance is never auto-approved
      -> `assess.test.ts` ("unresolvable targets escalate rather than pass")
  - a destructive command chained after a safe one does not slip through
      -> `assess.test.ts` / `gate.test.ts` ("chaining does not bypass the gate")
  - an inline shell script is assessed, not skipped
      -> `shellRisk.test.ts` ("inline shell scripts are assessed not skipped")
  - a known env variable does not exempt a protected path
      -> `assess.test.ts` ("known variables do not bypass protected paths")
  - safe commands pass straight through
      -> `gate.test.ts` ("safe commands pass straight through")
  - the union with the legacy denylist holds
      -> `riskTier.test.ts` (15 new assertions) + `shellRisk.test.ts` (11)

No network in tests: the classifier is pure string/path logic, no model and no I/O.

