`flexibleMatchHint` carries an "Adapted from jcode (MIT) ... edit.rs" header.

## Acceptance criteria
- **Reject empty old_string** -> "old_string must not be empty" (single) /
  "edit N: old_string must be non-empty" (batch); nothing written.
- **Reject old_string == new_string** -> reported as a no-op, nothing written.
- **Reject a non-unique match unless replace_all**, naming the count and both
  options: "old_string found 2 times. Either:\n1. Provide more context to make it
  unique, or\n2. Set replace_all: true to replace all occurrences".
- **Legacy `count` is preserved.** An explicit `count` on the single-edit path
  asserts the occurrence count in place of the uniqueness rule, and (as before
  this task) every occurrence is replaced; the count is verified against what was
  actually replaced before anything is written. Omitting `count` applies the spec
  rule (unique unless replace_all). See "Defects found and fixed" below: the first
  version of this port silently broke `count`, and that is now fixed and pinned
  by tests.
- **Miss hints** (`flexibleMatchHint`): trimming whitespace -> "found after
  trimming whitespace"; same lines modulo indentation -> "found with different
  indentation around line N" with the correct 1-based line; otherwise a plain
  "old_string not found in <path>. Use file_read ...". A miss is never
  fuzzy-applied: the original bytes are left untouched in every hint case.
- **Batch `edits[]`** validated atomically: applied in order to an in-memory copy,
  all-or-nothing, and every failure is reported ("No changes written to <path>.
  N of M edits failed:" + one `x ...` line per failure). Mixing `edits` with the
  single-edit shorthand is rejected; an empty or >50-item batch is rejected.
- **Post-edit context**: a line-numbered snippet of the file around the change
  (+/-3 lines, clamped to the file bounds), one per edit for a batch.

## Defects found and fixed while porting
**1. `new_string` was not substituted verbatim.** The first port used
`String.prototype.replace(str, str)`, which still expands `$&`, `` $` ``, `$'` and
`$$` in the replacement, whereas the spec substitutes new_string verbatim (Rust
`replacen`). A new_string containing `$&` was silently rewritten to the matched
text. Replaced with a slice-based literal substitution (the same approach the
sibling tools `multiEdit.ts` and `findAndEdit.ts` already use), and the
replacement's line count now follows Rust `str::lines` (a trailing newline starts
no further line) so the context window ends on the right line. Six regression
tests were added; the `$&` case was confirmed to fail against the previous
implementation.

**2. The legacy `count` parameter was silently broken by the port.** Routing the
single-edit path through `applyEditOperations` applied the spec uniqueness rule
unconditionally, so `count: 3` on a file with three occurrences was rejected as
non-unique. At the base commit the same call replaced all three (verified by
running the base and the port side by side). `count` is a documented parameter
that the port kept in the schema, so this was a public-contract break. An explicit
`count` now asserts the occurrence count in place of the uniqueness rule and
replaces every occurrence, exactly as before; omitting it keeps the spec rule.
Three regression tests were added and the `count: 3` case was confirmed to fail
against the broken implementation and to match the base behaviour after the fix.

